# ============================ INVENTORY 1 ============================
LESSONS_I1 = [
L("Chapter 1 — Warehouses: scope and record structure", "11", "<p>This module covers the Warehouse master and the stock architecture in ZhiftERP. On completing this module you will be able to: create Warehouse records with their full field set; design the warehouse tree with a naming convention; use the warehouse types and the special warehouses; wire the default warehouses across items and transactions; read the Bin record; and operate warehouse custody with named keepers.</p><p><b>What a warehouse is in the system.</b> A Warehouse record is a named place stock can be. It is not necessarily a building: a shelf area, a cold room, a van, a quarantine cage, and stock held at a subcontractor are all warehouses if the business needs a separate answer to two questions about them — what is here, and who answers for it. One physical building commonly contains several Warehouse records.</p><p><b>What reads the Warehouse record.</b></p><p>1. <b>Every stock transaction</b> — Purchase Receipts, Delivery Notes, Stock Entries, and Stock Reconciliations all post against warehouses; a movement is always from and/or to a named warehouse.<br>2. <b>The Stock Ledger Entry</b> — every posting writes ledger rows per item per warehouse (module 5 covers the ledger in full); the warehouse is one of the ledger's two primary dimensions.<br>3. <b>The Bin</b> — the standing per-item-per-warehouse balance record (Chapter 6).<br>4. <b>The stock reports</b> — Stock Balance, Stock Ledger, Stock Projected Qty, and the module 7 analytics all filter and group by warehouse.<br>5. <b>The accounting link</b> — under perpetual inventory, stock value posts to the account the warehouse resolves to (Chapter 2's account field; module 5 covers the postings).</p><p><b>Custody.</b> The stock function operates the records; each physical warehouse has a named keeper who answers for its contents (Chapter 7's custody list); movements between custodies are documents (module 2), and counts prove custody periodically (module 4). The standing programme rule applies from the first record: no stock change without its document.</p><p><b>Build order.</b> Warehouses are created before items carry defaults and before any stock posts, because everything above links to them:</p><p>1. The tree designed on paper first (Chapter 3) — levels, names, types.<br>2. Group nodes created, then leaf warehouses under them (Chapter 2).<br>3. The special warehouses added — transit, quarantine, subcontract (Chapter 4).<br>4. The defaults wired (Chapter 5).<br>5. The custody list written (Chapter 7).</p><p><b>Module contents.</b> Chapter 2: the Warehouse doctype field walk. Chapter 3: the tree design. Chapter 4: warehouse types and the special warehouses. Chapter 5: the default wiring. Chapter 6: the Bin. Chapter 7: custody and permissions. Chapter 8: worked example. Chapter 9: review and the module rule.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — The Warehouse doctype", "12", "<p>Open Warehouse (awesome bar) &gt; New. Complete the fields in order.</p><p><b>1. Warehouse Name.</b> The name per the convention Chapter 3 sets — the location prefix plus the function: <i>Central - Main Store</i>, <i>Ibadan - Stock Room</i>, <i>Central - Quarantine</i>. The system appends the company abbreviation to form the record ID; the name entered here is the readable part, and it prints on every stock document and report.</p><p><b>2. Company.</b> Warehouses are per company. On a multi-company site, each entity's stock lives in its own warehouse set; the legal separation rule applies to stock exactly as to everything else.</p><p><b>3. Parent Warehouse and Is Group.</b> Warehouses form a tree:</p><p>• <b>Is Group</b> ticked makes the record a grouping node — it can contain child warehouses and <b>cannot hold stock</b>.<br>• Leaf warehouses (Is Group clear) hold stock and appear on transactions.<br>• Select the Parent Warehouse to place the record in the tree; the Stock Balance report rolls leaf balances up through the group nodes.</p><p>The rule: stock sits only at leaf level. A group node that was created as a leaf and later needs children must first be emptied — plan the tree before stocking it (Chapter 3).</p><p><b>4. Warehouse Type.</b> The classification link (Chapter 4) — Stores, Work In Progress, Finished Goods, Transit, and the types the business defines. The type is a reporting and behaviour dimension; transit type in particular participates in the two-step transfer machinery (module 2, Chapter 4).</p><p><b>5. Account.</b> Under perpetual inventory, stock value posts to an account. The resolution order: the account set on this field, else the company's default inventory account. Set per-warehouse accounts only where the chart's design wants warehouse-level value visibility (the accountant's decision, recorded); otherwise leave the field blank and let the company default carry it — a mixed pattern set without a decision produces balance-sheet lines nobody can explain.</p><p><b>6. Address and contact.</b> Complete for physical locations that receive deliveries — the address prints on documents that route goods there.</p><p><b>7. Disabled.</b> Warehouses are retired by the Disabled flag, never deleted: ledger history references every warehouse that ever held stock, and the reference must stay readable. A warehouse is disabled only at zero stock — the closing procedure is: transfer the balance out by document, verify the Bin at zero, then disable.</p><p><b>Verification per record.</b> The name follows the convention; the company correct; the tree position correct with Is Group honest; the type selected; the account field per the recorded design; physical locations carrying addresses.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — The warehouse tree design", "12", "<p>The tree is designed once, on paper, before records are created. This chapter is the design procedure and its rules.</p><p><b>1. The design inputs.</b> List, per site the business operates: the physical rooms and areas that need separate accountability; the functions that need separation regardless of geography (quarantine, transit, subcontract); and who keeps each. The custody answer drives the structure: if two areas share one keeper and one answer, they are one warehouse; if one room has two accountable zones, it is two.</p><p><b>2. The level structure.</b> Three levels cover almost every business this track serves:</p><p>1. <b>The root</b> — All Warehouses (the system's root group node, per company).<br>2. <b>The site groups</b> — one group node per location: <i>Central</i>, <i>Ibadan</i>, <i>Akure</i>. Group nodes exist for rollup reporting: the Stock Balance report at the Ibadan node answers the whole branch in one row set.<br>3. <b>The leaf warehouses</b> — the accountability units under each site: <i>Central - Main Store</i>, <i>Central - Quarantine</i>, <i>Ibadan - Stock Room</i>.</p><p>Do not exceed three levels without a written reason. Every additional level adds a click to every lookup and a decision to every transaction, and the deep tree that mirrors shelf geography answers no question the leaf plus the item's own location note cannot.</p><p><b>3. The naming convention.</b> Written before the first record:</p><p>1. <b>Site prefix first</b> — every leaf name starts with its site: <i>Ibadan - ...</i>. Warehouse pickers on transactions sort and filter by name; the prefix keeps each site's warehouses adjacent and a mis-pick visible.<br>2. <b>Function second</b> — Main Store, Stock Room, Quarantine, Transit, Cold Room.<br>3. <b>No abbreviations that collide</b> — the convention document lists the allowed site codes.</p><p><b>4. The per-company rule.</b> On a multi-company site, each company gets its own tree from its own root. A shared physical building serving two entities holds two warehouse sets — the stock of each company in its own records, moved between companies only by the inter-company documents the Accounts track governs, never by an internal transfer that crosses a legal boundary.</p><p><b>5. The design review.</b> Before creation, the paper tree is reviewed against three tests:</p><p>1. <b>The custody test</b> — every leaf has exactly one named keeper (Chapter 7's list is drafted from the tree).<br>2. <b>The transaction test</b> — every planned movement type has a from and a to that exist (the receiving flow, the branch replenishment, the quarantine path, the transit legs — module 2's movements walked against the tree).<br>3. <b>The report test</b> — the questions the owner asks weekly (stock per branch, value per site, quarantine holdings) each answer from one node or one filter.</p><p>The tree that passes all three is created in an hour and rarely touched again; the tree improvised warehouse-by-warehouse is reorganised annually with stock in flight.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — Warehouse types and the special warehouses", "12", "<p>Warehouse Type records classify warehouses, and three special warehouses participate in specific machinery. This chapter covers both.</p><p><b>1. The Warehouse Type master.</b> Open Warehouse Type (awesome bar) &gt; New — short classification records: Stores, Work In Progress, Finished Goods, Transit, and any the business adds (Cold Chain, Quarantine). The type is selected on each Warehouse record (Chapter 2) and read by reports and by the transfer machinery below. Create the set once with the tree design.</p><p><b>2. The transit warehouse.</b> One transit warehouse per company (or per route where the business wants route-level visibility), type Transit. Its role in the two-step transfer (module 2, Chapter 4): goods leaving a source site post <b>into</b> transit; the receiving site's entry posts them <b>out of</b> transit into the destination. The consequences:</p><p>1. Stock physically on a truck is visible in the system as transit stock — the Stock Balance report filtered to the transit warehouse is the in-motion report.<br>2. Transit stock belongs to nobody's shelf count — counts at both sites reconcile because the moving stock sits in neither.<br>3. A transit balance older than the longest route is a shortfall or an unreceived transfer surfacing — module 2, Chapter 4's standing read.</p><p><b>3. The quarantine warehouse.</b> One per site that inspects or holds stock: type per the business's classification, name per the convention. Its role: goods that must not sell or issue — awaiting inspection, recalled, damaged pending decision — are moved there <b>by document</b> the moment the condition is known. The quarantine warehouse makes the hold visible and enforceable: the selling and fulfilment flows draw from the trading warehouses, and stock in quarantine is simply not there to pick. Module 3's recall procedure and module 2's damage lane both land stock here.</p><p><b>4. The subcontract warehouse.</b> Where the business sends materials to outside processors (the Procurement track's subcontracting flow), the materials remain the business's stock while at the vendor — held in a subcontract warehouse per vendor or one shared, per volume. The warehouse makes vendor-held stock a visible balance with a custody answer, counted by statement reconciliation with the vendor rather than by shelf count.</p><p><b>5. What does not get a warehouse.</b> Two non-cases, stated to prevent them:</p><p>1. <b>Customers' premises</b> — delivered goods left the business's stock at the Delivery Note; goods on approval or consignment follow the specific flows the Selling track defines, not an improvised customer warehouse per buyer.<br>2. <b>People</b> — a van or a technician's kit can be a warehouse (a place with custody); a person's informal holding cannot. Stock handed to a person without a document is module 2's first error, whatever it is called.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — The default warehouse wiring", "12", "<p>Transactions pre-fill their warehouses from a resolution chain. This chapter walks the chain and the wiring procedure. A default is a convenience with consequences: every mis-wired default posts silently to the wrong place until a count finds it.</p><p><b>1. The resolution order on a transaction row.</b> When a stock transaction line needs a warehouse, the system resolves, in order:</p><p>1. The warehouse typed on the row (always wins).<br>2. The document-level default (the warehouse set in the transaction's header, applied to new rows).<br>3. The <b>Item Default</b> for the item and company — the Default Warehouse row on the Item's defaults table.<br>4. The global default in <b>Stock Settings</b> (Default Warehouse).</p><p><b>2. The Item Defaults table.</b> On the Item record, one defaults row per company: the Default Warehouse (plus the accounts and cost-center defaults the Accounts track governs). Wire it per the item's home: fast-moving retail items to the site store that stocks them; centrally-held items to the central store. On a multi-site business where every site stocks the item, the item default carries the central home and the site documents override at header level — the convention is decided once and written, because a register wired half one way and half the other pre-fills wrongly somewhere every day.</p><p><b>3. The transaction-side defaults.</b></p><p>1. <b>Buying:</b> the Purchase Receipt's Set Accepted Warehouse header default; the supplier-facing flows land goods at the receiving site's store, with the Rejected Warehouse set to the quarantine warehouse where inspection is in use (the rejected line posts there automatically — the wiring that makes Chapter 4's quarantine real at the dock).<br>2. <b>Selling:</b> the Delivery Note and Sales Invoice source warehouse defaults; the POS Profile's warehouse (the ZhiftPOS track, module 2) fixes each till's source — a till pointing at the wrong warehouse sells one site's stock from another's records, and the POS profile review carries the check.<br>3. <b>Stock Settings:</b> the global Default Warehouse is the last resort of the chain — set it to the main central store and treat any posting that fell through to it as a wiring gap to fix, found by the monthly read below.</p><p><b>4. The wiring procedure.</b></p><p>1. At implementation: the convention written (which level carries which default), the Item Defaults loaded by governed import, the transaction and profile defaults set, Stock Settings last.<br>2. At item creation: the defaults row is part of the item-creation checklist — an item created without its warehouse default pre-fills from the global fallback and posts to the wrong store on its first receipt.<br>3. <b>The monthly read:</b> the Stock Ledger filtered to the global-default warehouse for the month — every line that landed there through the fallback names an item whose wiring is missing; fix the Item Default, and move the stock by document if it physically sits elsewhere.</p><p><b>Verification.</b> Sample five items per site: create a draft receipt and a draft delivery row for each and confirm the pre-filled warehouse is the intended one; delete the drafts. Ten minutes at go-live; repeated after any bulk item import.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — The Bin", "12", "<p>The Bin is the standing balance record — one per item per warehouse, maintained by the system from postings. This chapter covers reading it, the projected quantity, and the Bin's integrity.</p><p><b>1. What a Bin holds.</b> Open any item's record and view its stock levels, or open the Bin list filtered by item or warehouse. Per item-warehouse pair:</p><p>1. <b>Actual Qty</b> — the on-hand balance: the sum of every posted stock movement for the pair. This is the figure the shelf should match.<br>2. <b>Reserved Qty</b> — committed to submitted Sales Orders not yet delivered.<br>3. <b>Ordered Qty</b> — incoming on submitted Purchase Orders not yet received.<br>4. <b>Indented / Planned Qty</b> — requested on Material Requests / planned by production, where those flows are in use.<br>5. <b>Projected Qty</b> — the forward-looking figure: <i>actual + ordered − reserved</i> (plus the planning terms where used). Replenishment decisions (module 7) read projected, not actual — actual says what is on the shelf; projected says what will be after the commitments land.</p><p><b>2. How the Bin is maintained.</b> Every submitted stock document writes Stock Ledger Entries (module 5), and the Bin is the per-pair running total of those entries. Two consequences:</p><p>1. <b>The Bin is derived, never edited.</b> There is no lawful direct edit to a Bin figure. A wrong actual quantity is corrected by the document that explains it — a Stock Reconciliation after a count (module 4), or the missing movement entered (module 2). An edit without a document would make the balance disagree with its own ledger.<br>2. <b>The Bin and the ledger must agree.</b> They do, structurally, unless damage has occurred (a failed patch, a crashed repost); module 5 covers the integrity checks, and a bin-versus-ledger disagreement is an incident for the sysadmin lane, not a figure to adjust.</p><p><b>3. Reading bins in practice.</b></p><p>1. <b>The item view</b> — one item across all warehouses: where its stock sits, what is reserved where. The first lookup for any where-is-it question.<br>2. <b>The warehouse view</b> — the Stock Balance report at a warehouse: the keeper's own holdings list, and the count sheet's source (module 4).<br>3. <b>The negative check</b> — a negative Actual Qty means more left than arrived in the records: either a movement was never entered or stock was taken without document. Stock Settings' Allow Negative Stock switch decides whether the system permits the posting at all; the recommended posture is <b>off</b> (the honest register refuses to pretend), with module 8 covering the setting's full consequences. Where negatives exist from history, the standing read lists them and each is closed by its explaining document.</p><p><b>4. Reserved-quantity hygiene.</b> Reserved figures come from submitted Sales Orders; orders that will never fulfil (stale, cancelled by conversation but not in the system) hold reservations that distort projected quantities and block replenishment arithmetic. The monthly read: reservations against orders older than the fulfilment horizon, each closed or re-dated by its document — the Selling track's order hygiene, read from the stock side.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — Custody and permissions", "12", "<p>Stock is money on shelves. This chapter sets who can do what to it, and who answers for each place it sits.</p><p><b>1. The custody list.</b> One page, drafted from the tree (Chapter 3), maintained by the stock function:</p><p>• One row per leaf warehouse: the warehouse, the named keeper, the deputy, and the count cadence (module 4 sets the cadences).<br>• The keeper answers for the warehouse's contents: receives against documents, releases against documents, and signs the counts. The deputy covers absence — a warehouse whose keeper is on leave with no deputy is module 4's unmarked-location failure in stock form.<br>• The list is reviewed quarterly and at every staffing change; a leaver or mover on the custody list is a same-day update (the HR track's lifecycle events carry the line).</p><p><b>2. The role design.</b> Per the Sysadmin track's permission rules, the stock roles at their standard shape:</p><p>1. <b>Stock User</b> — creates and submits the daily movement documents (entries, receipts, deliveries) within their site's scope.<br>2. <b>Stock Manager</b> — the elevated set: reconciliations, backdated postings where permitted, cancellations and amendments, the settings pages. The count-adjustment power in particular sits here, because a reconciliation moves value with one document (module 4's controls).<br>3. <b>Read-only stock access</b> — the reporting roles for finance and management.</p><p>The separation rule: <b>the keeper of a warehouse does not approve their own adjustments.</b> Counts are entered by the counting procedure's rules (module 4 — counted by one, entered against the sheet, the variance approved by the stock manager or owner per the threshold), so no single person can both hold the stock and rewrite its record.</p><p><b>3. Site scoping.</b> Where the business wants branch users confined to branch warehouses, user permissions on the Warehouse doctype scope each user to their site's records — the branch storekeeper sees and posts to Ibadan warehouses only. Apply scoping per the sysadmin track's user-permission procedure and record it in the permission matrix; the quarterly access recertification covers the stock roles and scopes with everything else.</p><p><b>4. The document rule, restated as custody's mechanics.</b> Custody changes only by document:</p><p>1. Into the business: Purchase Receipt (from suppliers), Stock Entry Material Receipt (the documented non-purchase cases).<br>2. Between custodies: Stock Entry Material Transfer (module 2) — the sending keeper's release and the receiving keeper's acceptance are the document's two sides.<br>3. Out of the business: Delivery Note (to customers), Stock Entry Material Issue with reason and approval (consumption, damage, write-off — module 2).<br>4. Nothing moves on a conversation. The stock handed over to be sorted out later is the origin of most variances module 4 will ever find; the document takes ninety seconds and the variance investigation takes a day.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Worked example: designing HealthTrade's network", "13", "<p>This chapter applies Chapters 2-7 to HealthTrade (the pharmaceutical distributor: Central/Lagos with the main operation, branches at Ibadan and Akure). Configuration values and outcomes are listed per step.</p><p><b>Step 1 — the design inputs and the tree (Chapter 3).</b> The custody walk of the physical sites lists: at Central — the main store, the cold room, the quarantine cage, the dispatch bay; at each branch — one stock room. The paper tree, three levels nowhere exceeded:</p><p>• All Warehouses (root)<br>• Central (group): <i>Central - Main Store</i>, <i>Central - Cold Room</i>, <i>Central - Quarantine</i>, <i>Central - Dispatch</i><br>• Ibadan (group): <i>Ibadan - Stock Room</i><br>• Akure (group): <i>Akure - Stock Room</i><br>• Plus the specials at root level: <i>Transit - HT</i> (type Transit), <i>Subcontract - Repack Vendor</i> (the outside repacker holding HealthTrade stock).</p><p>The three design tests run: custody — every leaf one keeper (eight rows drafted); transactions — receiving, branch replenishment via transit, the quarantine path, and the subcontract leg each have their from and to; reports — stock per branch answers at the site group nodes, quarantine holdings at one leaf.</p><p><b>Step 2 — the records (Chapter 2).</b> Warehouse Types confirmed (Stores, Transit, plus Cold Chain added); the group nodes created, then the leaves, names per the convention (site prefix, function). The account field left blank on all — the company default inventory account carries value, recorded as the accountant's decision. The dispatch bay is created as a leaf deliberately: loaded goods awaiting the truck have their own answer, separate from the main store's count.</p><p><b>Step 3 — the defaults wired (Chapter 5).</b> Item Defaults loaded by governed import: retail pharmaceuticals default to <i>Central - Main Store</i>; cold-chain items to <i>Central - Cold Room</i>. The Purchase Receipt's Rejected Warehouse set to <i>Central - Quarantine</i>. The branch POS profiles' warehouses confirmed at their own stock rooms. Stock Settings' global default set to the main store; the monthly fallback read scheduled. The five-item pre-fill verification runs per site — one miss found (a cold-chain item defaulting to the main store; corrected before go-live rather than by July's count).</p><p><b>Step 4 — custody and roles (Chapter 7).</b> The custody list finalised: eight leaves, eight keepers, deputies named for the three sites. The replenishment policy written into the procedures: <b>branches replenish by transfer only</b> — branch stock arrives from Central through the transit warehouse, never by direct branch purchasing outside the procurement lanes. Branch storekeepers scoped to their site's warehouses by user permission; Stock Manager held by two people at Central; the keeper-does-not-approve-own-adjustments separation recorded in the matrix.</p><p><b>Result.</b> Ten warehouses, three levels, one written convention, eight custody rows, defaults verified by the pre-fill sample, and the special paths (quarantine, transit, subcontract) existing before the first document needs them. Module 2 runs a month of movements across exactly this network.</p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common errors and the module rule", "11", "<p>The errors that recur in warehouse architecture, each with its correction and prevention.</p><p><b>Error 1 — the person-shaped warehouse.</b> Symptom: stock handed to people without documents; \"with Emeka\" as a location; variances at every count. Correction: the informal holdings documented into real warehouses or issued properly. Prevention: the custody rule — a place with a keeper can be a warehouse; an undocumented handover cannot, and nothing moves on a conversation.</p><p><b>Error 2 — the geography tree.</b> Symptom: five-level trees mirroring shelves and aisles; every transaction a navigation exercise; reports that need six nodes to answer one branch. Correction: the tree flattened to the accountability units, stock transferred by document into the merged leaves, emptied leaves disabled. Prevention: the three-level rule and the three design tests before creation.</p><p><b>Error 3 — stock on group nodes and deleted warehouses.</b> Symptom: attempts to post to group nodes; warehouses deleted with history and reports breaking. Correction: reposting to leaves; deleted records restored per the incident lane. Prevention: Is Group honest from creation, and retirement by the Disabled flag at zero balance only — never deletion.</p><p><b>Error 4 — the mis-wired defaults.</b> Symptom: receipts landing in the wrong store; a till selling from another site's records; the global fallback collecting strays for months. Correction: the Item Defaults and profile warehouses fixed; misplaced stock moved by document. Prevention: the wiring convention written once, the item-creation checklist carrying the defaults row, the five-item pre-fill sample at go-live, and the monthly fallback read.</p><p><b>Error 5 — the edited balance.</b> Symptom: someone \"fixing\" a quantity outside the document flow; the balance disagreeing with its own ledger. Correction: the change reversed and re-entered as the document that explains it (the reconciliation or the missing movement). Prevention: the rule that the Bin is derived, never edited — a wrong figure is corrected by a document, and bin-versus-ledger disagreement is a sysadmin incident.</p><p><b>Error 6 — the custody list nobody keeps.</b> Symptom: warehouses without keepers; keepers who left months ago; counts nobody signs. Correction: the list rebuilt from the tree with current names. Prevention: the quarterly review and the lifecycle-event line — every transfer, promotion, or exit that touches a keeper updates the list the same day.</p><p><b>Review list.</b> Before the quiz, confirm you can: state what a warehouse is and what reads it; create a Warehouse record with the full field walk including the account resolution and the disable-at-zero rule; design a three-level tree through the three tests with a written naming convention; use the transit, quarantine, and subcontract warehouses and state what each enables; wire the default chain — row, header, Item Default, Stock Settings — with the monthly fallback read; read a Bin including projected quantity, and state why bins are never edited; and operate the custody list with the role separation and site scoping.</p><p><b>Module rule 1: a warehouse is a named accountability unit — designed into a shallow tree by written convention, stocked only at leaf level, wired into defaults once and verified, held by a named keeper, and changed only by document.</b> Module 2 covers the Stock Entry and the internal movements.</p>"),
]

